# Exercise: Starting a TCP Listener
#
# `net.tcp.listen` opens a TCP socket bound to a local port and
# starts listening for incoming connections.
#
# Stack effect: ( Int -- Socket Bool )
#   ( port -- socket success )
#
# Like every other word in net.tcp, listen ends with a success Bool
# so the caller decides how to handle failure. Listen fails when the
# port is already in use, when you don't have permission to bind it,
# or when the socket registry is full.
#
# Idiomatic shape — `[ ok-branch ] [ err-branch ] if`:
#
#     8080 net.tcp.listen
#     [ # ( socket )
#       net.tcp.close drop
#     ]
#     [ # the socket value on failure is junk; just drop it
#       drop
#     ]
#     if
#
# Your task: fill in the success branch so that the test asserts
# success after a clean listen-then-close round-trip.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-listen ( -- )
    18261 net.tcp.listen
    [ # ( socket )  -- write your code here:
      #   close the socket, drop the close-success Bool,
      #   then leave `true` for the assertion below.
      drop false
    ]
    [ drop false ]
    if

    # Do not edit below this line
    test.assert
;
